    meditation: `Open the week with the most uncompromising sentence ever written by an abbot to a young monk. Even from good things, Benedict says, we ought sometimes to keep silence. Not from idle chatter only, not from gossip and slander, not from the lie and the half-truth — those go without saying. From good things. From words that would, in themselves, edify. From the right answer you could give. From the point you could score. From the doctrine you could state correctly. Even from those, sometimes, you should hold your peace.

The chapter is called De Taciturnitate, on taciturnity, and it is the sixth chapter of a Rule that has shaped Western religious life for fifteen hundred years. Benedict is not theorizing. He is writing for a community of men whose work, in a literal sense, was speech — chant, prayer, reading aloud, instruction of novices — and he is telling them that the discipline of restraint must precede and surround the discipline of utterance. The monk who has not learned to keep his mouth shut at the right moment has not yet learned to open it.

Hear this as a lawyer.

Your profession is the modern world's most articulate. You were chosen, in part, for verbal aptitude; you were trained, for years, in the precise deployment of language; you are paid, every day, to say things on behalf of others who cannot say them for themselves. Your work product is words. Your reputation is built on the persuasiveness of your words. Your billing, in some shops, is measured in tenths of an hour of your words. There is no profession in which the temptation to speak too much is more structural.

And there is no profession, perhaps, where the cost of speaking too much is higher. The unguarded sentence in the deposition that reopened the door you had spent six months closing. The off-the-record remark to the reporter that ran on the front page. The text to opposing counsel sent in the heat of midnight that you spent the next morning trying to claw back. The hallway boast that found its way to the partner who had not yet decided about you. The witness coaching that crossed a line you did not see at the time because you were doing it in your normal voice. Most lawyers can name three of these from their own career within ten minutes of being asked. Benedict's chapter is written for the man who keeps producing the fourth.

The Psalm Benedict quotes — the thirty-eighth — is the Psalter's interior monologue of a man who has decided to put a guard on his own mouth. I said, I will take heed to my ways, that I sin not with my tongue. I have set a watch to my mouth, when the sinner stood against me. He was dumb, the Psalmist says, and was humbled, and kept silence even from good things. There is a peculiar humility in the choice of silence. Speech is the way we assert ourselves in a room. To choose not to speak, when you could have spoken truly and well, is to consent to be smaller than the conversation. It is to let the room go on without your contribution. The Psalmist did this not because his words were bad but because the discipline of withholding them sometimes serves something larger than getting them out.

This is what Benedict means by taciturnitas. It is not the silence of the man who has nothing to say. It is the silence of the man who has plenty to say and has chosen, this hour, not to say it. The first is dullness. The second is virtue.

Sirach, in tomorrow's tradition, said it earlier: a wise man will hold his peace till he see opportunity. The wise man does not refuse speech. He waits for it. He waits for the moment when speech will be heard, when it will accomplish what it can accomplish, when it will not merely fill the air. He has trained his patience to outlast the others' impatience. Most rooms reward the first man who speaks. The wise man has decided to be the last.

There is a practical curriculum hidden in this opening day, and Benedict would want it stated plainly. Begin to notice the moments when you spoke and ought not to have. Not the moments when you lied or gossiped or boasted — those are obvious, and you already confess them — but the moments when what you said was true and edifying and well-aimed, and the speaking of it nevertheless made the room worse. The unsought legal opinion offered at a dinner party. The correction administered to a junior associate in front of his peers when it could have waited until Tuesday. The doctrinal point made to your spouse during what was, until that moment, an ordinary marital disagreement. None of these was a sin in the catechetical sense. All of them were failures of taciturnitas.

The Rule wants to retrain your ear. Most lawyers, asked at the end of a day what they said that they regret, will name the indiscretions. The deeper question Benedict asks is what you said today that was true, accurate, and yet need not have been said at all. The volume of those, summed over a week, is usually larger than the volume of the obvious sins of the tongue. They are the small leaks in the bucket the contemplatives are trying to keep full.

Sit with one more sentence. Let leave to speak be seldom granted even to perfect disciples. Even to perfect disciples. Even to the man who has worked at this for thirty years. Benedict is realistic about how easily the tongue resumes its old habits the moment the discipline is relaxed. He is realistic, also, about how the perfect disciple's speech will be heavier because it is rarer. Words from a man who speaks seldom carry weight that words from the constantly speaking man cannot carry. Save your weight for the case.

Tomorrow Aquinas will give you the metaphysics of this discipline. Today, simply receive the Rule's first instruction, and try it for one day. Not for a week, not for the rest of your life — Benedict is patient about lapses. For today. Notice once, when you are about to say the true thing that need not be said, and do not say it. Notice the small change in the air of the room. Notice that the room did not require you. Notice that you survived not being required. Carry the noticing into Friday's cross-examination, where it will become craft.

The disciplined tongue does not begin in the courtroom. It begins on the way into the office, in a small choice not to comment on the parking, not to text the witty rejoinder, not to circulate the article that proved you right. The cathedral of taciturnity is built one withheld sentence at a time.`,
