    meditation: `James says the tongue no man can tame. Aquinas, who read James as carefully as anyone in the Western tradition, is not telling you to despair of taming it. He is telling you what taming it actually looks like, and where it sits in the structure of the virtues.

Open the Summa to question 111 of the secunda secundae. The treatise on the moral virtues has been moving slowly through the cardinal virtues; now, in the long territory annexed to justice, Aquinas reaches the virtue of truth and its surrounding country. Truth, for him, is a virtue distinct from but related to justice — the virtue by which a man's outward signs correspond to his inward reality. Its opposed vices are lying, in question 110, and dissimulation and hypocrisy, in question 111. Question 114 will return to a different but related thing, the virtue of friendliness or affability that governs everyday speech. The cluster matters. Aquinas thinks of speech the way a craftsman thinks of an apprentice's hands: the work is done well only when many habits have been formed together.

Read the passage above carefully. Aquinas distinguishes two faults in the way we manifest ourselves to others. The first is the obvious one. We say something that is not so. We claim a credential we do not hold. We attribute to ourselves a virtue we have not earned. We hide a fact whose absence will mislead. That is the vice of lying, and the lawyer's professional ethics have a hundred rules against it, most of them well-drafted.

The second fault is the one the rules do not cover so neatly. We use external signs — words or deeds, but also tones, postures, dress, silences — that do not correspond to the thing signified or to our condition. Not lying, in the strict sense, but disssembling. Producing a signal that is not false but is also not accurate. The veteran trial lawyer who walks into the deposition with a slight smile that is meant to suggest he knows something he does not in fact know. The expert witness who is technically truthful in every sentence but whose total demeanor implies a certainty his report did not earn. The press release whose every clause is defensible but whose effect on the ordinary reader is a misimpression. The witness coaching session that never quite tells the client to lie but somehow always produces a witness whose testimony is shaped against the gravity of the underlying facts.

Aquinas calls this dissimulatio and hypocrisis. The English words have hardened into specific contemporary meanings. The Latin keeps the older breadth. Dissimulation is the production of a sign that does not befit the inward thing. Hypocrisy is the actor's vice, the playing of a role on a stage when the room is not a stage. Both are species of the offense against truth that is not technically a lie.

This is the territory of the disciplined tongue that the Christian lawyer must learn to govern. The professional ethics rules will handle the perjury. The Holy Spirit, working with your formed conscience, must handle the dissimulation.

What does formation look like? Begin with question 109, where Aquinas has defined the virtue of truth. The virtuous man, he says, manifests himself as he is, neither more nor less. He is not constantly volunteering his interior — that would be inappropriate, indecent, sometimes imprudent — but when he does manifest something, the manifestation corresponds to what is. There is a wholeness, a congruentia, between the inner and the outer. The disciplined tongue is one symptom of this wholeness. It says what is, when saying serves, with no more weight than the matter bears and no less than it requires.

Now look at question 114, on affability — the virtue Aquinas considers a part of justice because it concerns what we owe one another in the daily conversation of common life. Affability is the steady virtue of bringing into ordinary speech the appropriate honor, mildness, and respect that another person is owed. The vice opposed by defect is moroseness; the vice opposed by excess is adulation. Affability is the middle — the rendering, in the small currency of words, of what each person is due.

Put these together. The Christian lawyer's disciplined tongue is not a matter of saying as little as possible. It is a matter of saying what is, in a way that befits the matter and the person, without the dissimulation that adds spin to truth and without the moroseness that withholds the honor due. The Pauline command — let your speech be seasoned with salt — is exactly this craft. The lawyer's words, if they are seasoned with salt, will still mean what they meant when the file is reopened in five years.

What does this require in practice? It requires you to notice the small dissimulations. The press release that overstates the verdict. The pleading that strategically omits the case that is on point against you. The cross-examination question whose form implies a fact you cannot actually prove. Each of these, in isolation, can be defended within the rules. Each is a small piece of dissimulation as Aquinas defines it. They accumulate. The veteran lawyer who has practiced this way for twenty years has formed a habit of making outward signs that do not correspond to the inward thing. He may not be lying in the technical sense at any moment. He has nevertheless become a man whose speech does not bear the salt.

This is the harder discipline, and it is why Benedict's chapter on silence stands at the head of the week. The first defense against dissimulation is not eloquence. It is restraint. The man who says fewer things has fewer chances to misalign his outward sign with his inward truth. The man who waits before speaking has time for the inward thing to settle so that the outward sign can match it. The disciplined tongue begins in the disciplined pause.

Aquinas would close the question with the small reminder he often makes: virtue is in habits, habits are in repeated acts, repeated acts must be chosen at first against the grain of habit not yet formed. You will not become a man of truthful speech by reading the question. You will become one by catching, this week, two or three small dissimulations and refusing them. The press conference you do not embellish. The deposition smile you do not affect. The half-truth in the email you rewrite. Each is a small repetition, and Aquinas is patient about the years it takes.

The tongue no man can tame, says James. Aquinas: not by the tongue's own efforts. But by the grace of God working through a slow formation of mind and will and habit, the tongue can be governed enough that the lawyer's signs come, at last, to correspond to the things signified. That is what it means, for a man whose work is words, to have learned at last to tell the truth.`,
