    meditation: `Ambrose was a Roman lawyer before he was a bishop. He had argued before tribunals and sat on a provincial governor's bench. When he wrote De Officiis for the clergy of Milan, he wrote in the conscious shadow of Cicero's book of the same name, and he reached, very early in the first book, for the topic of silence. He did this for a reason. He had been the man who had to learn the art the hard way, in chambers full of advocates who had not learned it, and he knew that the cleric's pulpit and the lawyer's tribunal are nearer to each other than either profession likes to admit.

Today's meditation is on silence in chambers — the room behind the courtroom, the conference room at mediation, the small office where the judge and the lawyers gather to take the temperature of the matter without the record running. It is also on the kindred silences of the office: the partners' meeting, the client conference, the post-mortem after the bad ruling. Chambers stands for any room where you are tempted to argue when you ought not.

Begin with what the temptation feels like. The judge has just made a remark you disagree with. It was a stray remark, an aside, perhaps wrong on the law but not yet a ruling. Everyone in the room heard it. Your client heard it. Opposing counsel heard it. The silence that follows is the silence of a small contested space. Will you correct him? Will you let it pass? Will you smile faintly and move on?

Most young lawyers correct. They were trained to. They were taught in moot court that the lawyer who lets a misstatement of the law stand has failed his client. They were taught in the bar examiner's hypothetical that the diligent advocate raises the point. The instinct is honorable. It is also, often, wrong.

Ambrose's first sentence is the beginning of the corrective. Let us guard our hearts, let us guard our mouth. The order matters. The guard on the mouth is downstream of the guard on the heart. The lawyer who does not first ask what is moving in him when the judge speaks will not know whether the next sentence out of his mouth is the law speaking through him or his vanity speaking around the law. Ambrose, who had been a magistrate, knew that the line between juridical zeal and self-display is often invisible to the man practicing it.

So a first discipline. When the judge has said something you disagree with, count. Count to five before you speak. In those five seconds, ask: is the misstatement going into the ruling, or was it dictum? Is correction necessary now, or can it be put in the post-hearing brief without losing the point? What does my client gain if I am right in this exchange, and what does he lose if I appear to be the kind of advocate who must always correct the bench? Five seconds is enough for most of these questions. By the time the five seconds is up, three out of four lawyers will know that the better answer is the next sentence the judge speaks, not the next sentence they were about to make.

This is one specific form of silence in chambers. There are others.

There is the silence when the judge has signaled, gently, that he is leaning your way. The temptation here is to keep arguing — to drive the point home, to bury the opposition, to be sure that the favorable inclination becomes a favorable ruling. Veteran lawyers will tell you to resist. The judge who is leaning toward you wants you to stop talking so he can do the work of writing the ruling in his head. Every additional sentence you add risks the small displeasure that turns a soft favor into a closer call. The disciplined advocate, when he sees the judge leaning his way, says one more clean sentence and sits down.

There is the silence when opposing counsel has overargued and is doing your work for him. He is making the case look weaker by his repetition. He is alienating the bench by his tone. The silence here is the silence of restraint that lets the room finish its own calculation. Do not interrupt. Do not move to clarify. Let him go. The judge is forming a view, and the view is favorable to your client because the other lawyer cannot stop talking. Your competence in this moment is shown by absence.

There is the silence when your client, beside you, leans over and whispers a directive you cannot follow — to interrupt, to object, to make the dramatic gesture. The disciplined response is not the public refusal. It is the small nod that buys the moment, and the considered choice afterward not to do what you cannot do. The client is paying for your judgment, not for his.

There is the silence when a senior colleague is about to make a mistake in the room and you can see it coming. Sometimes you intervene. Often you do not. The senior colleague's authority is the structure within which your participation is permitted, and the petty correction in front of others is corrosive of an authority you need preserved. Note the matter. Raise it after, privately. Hold the page closed.

Ambrose's deeper claim is the line that gives the day its weight: to learn to be silent that we might know how to speak, is no small matter; for he who knows not how to be silent knows not, either, how to speak. The two competences are reciprocal. The lawyer who cannot keep silence cannot speak well. His arguments are louder than they need to be, his rhetoric more ornate, because he has not learned to trust the unspoken pause to do part of the work. The advocate whose silences are well-placed has more weight when he speaks. The whole room calibrates to him without his needing to raise his voice.

This is the chambers practice you are aiming at. Count to five before you correct the bench. Stop talking when the judge is leaning your way. Let opposing counsel finish his own collapse. Hold the file closed when the colleague is going wrong in a way you can repair after. Say no comment to the reporter and walk past.

None of this is dramatic. None of this is the kind of thing a trial advocacy course teaches. All of it is what Ambrose, the bishop who had been a magistrate, would have wanted you to know: that the man who knows how to be silent in chambers is the one who, when at last he speaks, will be the one the room hears.

Chambers tomorrow will reward you for what you do not say today.`,
