    meditation: `There is a rule of cross-examination that veteran trial lawyers pass down to associates the way medieval guilds passed down trade secrets. It has many forms. The simplest is: do not ask a question to which you do not already know the answer. The deeper form, the one only experience teaches, is: do not ask the question whose answer you do know, when the asking will get you less than the silence would.

This is the silence of cross. Today's meditation sits with it.

Begin with what cross-examination actually is. It is not, despite the romance of the trial movie, the moment when the lawyer breaks the witness and the witness confesses. That happens roughly never. Cross-examination is a structured exchange in which the advocate, having prepared, walks the witness across a small number of carefully chosen factual points that, taken together, support the closing argument the advocate will give the jury two days later. The points are usually undramatic. The witness usually does not capitulate. The advocate is building, brick by brick, a wall the witness cannot remove, by asking questions whose answers he has already pinned down through depositions, documents, and prior statements.

The skill of cross is the skill of selection. There are fifty questions you could ask. You ask twelve. The other thirty-eight are the questions you chose not to ask. The discipline of cross is in the silences.

Some of the unasked questions are obvious. You do not ask the open-ended question that lets the witness explain. You do not ask the question that would let the witness reach for the document that hurts you. You do not ask the question that gives the witness the opportunity to apologize sympathetically to the jury. These are first-year lessons. The more interesting silences are the ones the curriculum does not teach you.

The first is the question whose answer you already know and whose asking will only soften it. The witness has admitted, in deposition, a fact that hurts him. The fact is in your transcript. You will read it to the jury in closing. There is a temptation, on cross, to make the witness say it again in person, on the stand, in front of the jurors. Sometimes you should. Often you should not. The witness, on the stand, will phrase the admission with qualifications he did not bother with in deposition. He will say yes, but. He will look apologetic and earnest. He will introduce a verbal nuance that, while not denying the fact, blunts its edge. You went into the question with a sharp instrument and you came out with the same instrument slightly dulled. Better, sometimes, to leave the admission in the cold typed deposition and let it sit, undefended, when you read it aloud in closing. The witness's absence at that moment is louder than his presence would have been.

The second silence is the unasked question because you already know the answer would surprise you. You did the work. You read the documents. You wrote out the cross. There is one question you considered putting on the list and pulled, because you cannot quite predict how the witness will answer it, and the variance is bad for your case more often than it is good. You leave it off. You move past it. The jury does not know there was a question you skipped. Only you and your second chair know. The discipline of having pulled it is the kind of restraint Cicero meant when he said the well-bred man neither breaks in nor lets the conversation stray too far. You kept the cross on the line.

The third silence is the question you do not ask because the answer, while favorable to you, would also be damaging in a way you do not need. The witness would admit that his employer has lied to him too. The admission would help your impeachment of the employer. It would also implicate a third party your client does not want implicated. You leave it. Cross-examination is not the maximization of damage. It is the careful selection of damage in service of a closing argument that has to add up to something coherent.

The fourth silence is the question you do not ask because you do not need it. The case is going well. The witness has been competent and unhelpful to himself. You have made the four points you came to make. There is a fifth point you could press. You sit down. The senior lawyers in the courtroom will know, by the early sit-down, that you knew what you came for and got it. The jury will register the discipline. You will have walked out by the path Cicero recommends — keeping the conversation on its line.

Now turn this into a habit broader than cross. The intake call where you do not ask the client the question that would force him to lie to you. The discovery deposition where you do not chase the rabbit because the rabbit is the other side's, not yours. The settlement conference where you do not ask the mediator to confirm what the other side just offered. Each requires the same competence: knowing what you do not need to know to do your job, and choosing not to ask.

This raises a question Aquinas began to address yesterday. When is the unspoken question dissimulation, and when is it the licit silence of the advocate doing his job? Aquinas's answer, in shorthand, is that the lawyer is not bound to volunteer every fact known to him; he is bound not to make signs that misrepresent. The unasked question is not a sign at all. It is the absence of one. The judge and jury reach conclusions on the evidence put before them. The silences are part of the structure, not violations of it.

The discipline of the unasked question, then, is one of the deepest professional skills the trial lawyer ever acquires, and it is hard to teach because it is taught only by years of restraint. The young associate will ask too much on cross because he cannot bear to leave a known weakness alone. The veteran will leave it. The veteran is, in this sense, the closer of Cicero's well-bred man — the conversationalist who keeps the talk on its line, who knows when the room has heard what it needs to hear, and who declines to fill the remaining minutes with sound.

There is a small spiritual discipline that prepares you for the courtroom one. In conversation tonight, with a colleague, your spouse, a friend at dinner, notice once that you are about to ask a question whose answer you do not need. Notice that the asking would be for your sake, not the other's. Notice that the silence would serve them better than the question. Let the silence stand. See what fills it. You will be practicing, in miniature, the discipline that wins the cross-examination two months from now.`,
