    meditation: `The press conference you did not give is the one your client will be grateful for, two years on, when the case is over and the file is closed and the only public record of the matter is what got filed in court. Today's meditation is on the silence you keep with the press. It is the most-asked and least-practiced silence in modern law.

Begin with the structure. A reporter calls. Sometimes it is a beat reporter who covers your court. Sometimes it is a national reporter who has gotten your name from a partner's profile. Sometimes it is a producer building a segment. They want a comment. They have a deadline. They will write the piece with or without you. The implicit pressure is real. If you say nothing, the piece runs without your perspective, and your client's position is told by the other side, the prosecutor's office, the regulator, the plaintiff's PR firm. If you say something, you risk saying the wrong thing, the thing that gets quoted out of context, the thing that becomes the headline. The space between is the lawyer's no comment, and it is harder to hold than it looks.

The first thing to know is that no comment is not silence. It is a phrase. It is a structured, considered, professional response that says: I am not making a public statement at this time. It is a sign, in Aquinas's sense, that corresponds to an inward reality — the reality that this matter is being litigated in the proper forum, which is the court, not the newspaper. The reporter may receive no comment as evasion. The lawyer must train herself to deliver it as integrity. The two readings of the same phrase are very different in effect, and the difference is in the delivery, which is in the formation of the lawyer who delivers it.

Newman, in The Idea of a University, gives us the deeper grammar. The gentleman, he writes, is one who never inflicts pain. He removes the obstacles that hinder the free movement of others, rather than imposing his own will on the room. Newman is not writing about lawyers. He is sketching the temperament of the educated Christian as a kind of standing furniture for the lives of others — the easy chair, the warm fire — present and useful without imposing. Carry this into the press conference. The lawyer who refuses to comment on the pending case is, in Newman's idiom, declining to inflict pain. He is declining to add a voice to a public discussion that is, structurally, ill-suited to do justice to the matter. He is leaving the field clear for the proceeding that is actually equipped to do justice — the trial, the hearing, the appeal — to do its work.

What about the temptation to defend the client in the court of public opinion? Some cases call for it. Most do not. The lawyer who tries to litigate in the press will, ninety times out of a hundred, do more harm than good. The press has its own rhythms, its own appetites, its own incentives, and its own time horizon, none of which is the time horizon of the case. A quote that wins the news cycle on Tuesday may close the door on a plea negotiation on Friday. A press conference that satisfies the client's hunger to be heard may give the prosecutor exactly the inflammatory pre-trial publicity she will use to seat a less sympathetic jury. The press is not a tool you control. It is a current you can ride briefly, and that will, more often, carry you somewhere you did not want to go.

There are exceptions. There are matters of genuine public interest in which silence by counsel allows a misinformation to harden into accepted fact. There are clients whose only protection is the visibility their lawyer can give them. There are cases where the press is the last remaining avenue for justice. In those cases, you speak — carefully, with prepared talking points, after consultation with your client and any consultants you have retained, and with the awareness that everything you say will be in the record. But these cases are the exception. The default is no comment, and the default has to be defended, sometimes, against the client's instinct that not commenting is the lawyer's cowardice.

The client's instinct here deserves a paragraph. Clients in high-profile matters often feel, with reason, that they are being publicly tried by a press they cannot answer. They want their lawyer to fight on every front. They cannot understand, in the moment, why you are refusing to talk to the reporter who called. The discipline of the lawyer is to explain — patiently, perhaps repeatedly — that the courtroom is the forum, and that what you do not say is part of what you do for them. The lawyer who is too eager to please by holding the press conference has substituted PR for representation. The client may thank you. The trial judge will not.

There is a structural category of press silence too. Model Rule 3.6 and its state analogues limit out-of-court statements that have a substantial likelihood of materially prejudicing an adjudicative proceeding. The rule is a floor, not a ceiling. The Christian lawyer, formed by taciturnitas, should aspire to more silence than the rule strictly requires.

One last form of press silence deserves attention. There is the omission in the press release. The release goes out from the firm or from the agency. It contains accurate sentences. The accurate sentences, taken together, give an impression that is not quite accurate. The omission is the part of the matter the release does not mention — the unfavorable ruling earlier in the case, the prior settlement that did not go well, the regulatory action that is still pending. The release is technically true. It is also, in Aquinas's sense, a dissimulation. The Christian lawyer who is asked to review the release before it goes out is being asked to participate in the construction of the dissimulating sign. Sometimes the lawyer must, gently and firmly, insist on the additional sentence — the one that makes the picture honest, even at some cost to the client's narrative. The cost of refusing to insist on it is the slow corrosion of a habit. The lawyer who has signed off on five press releases that left out the inconvenient sentence has formed himself into a person whose signature does not mean what it should mean. Most lawyers do not notice the formation. The formation has happened anyway.

The final word on press silence is the simplest. The case will outlast the news cycle. The file will sit on a shelf in the records room for seven years before destruction. The reporter who wanted the comment will have moved on to three other stories by the time you next think about her. The client will, in eighteen months, be on the next chapter of his life. What will remain, after all of this, is the question: did you say what was true, when it served, and keep silent when it would not? The lawyer who can answer yes to both, even in the years of pressure to do otherwise, has kept faith with an older profession than the news cycle.

No comment, said well, is the gentleman's sentence in the press's hall. It is a small refusal of pain. It is a vote of confidence in the courtroom downstairs. It is the silence by which the trial is preserved as the forum competent to do what the press cannot.`,
